<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1861</h1><p class="ref-lede">1861 in world history: 59 events, 24 births, 12 deaths, 2 works, 6 states founded and 9 that came to an end. 1,052 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a><a href="/years/1858">1858</a><a href="/years/1859">1859</a><a href="/years/1860">1860</a><a href="/years/1861" aria-current="page">1861</a><a href="/years/1862">1862</a><a href="/years/1863">1863</a><a href="/years/1864">1864</a><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Cadets fire on the Star of the West in Charleston Harbor</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Southern guns fired on the merchant ship Star of the West as it tried to resupply Fort Sumter, forcing it to turn back. The shots are sometimes called the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Florida secedes from the Union</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>'s convention voted 62 to 7 to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the third state to do so after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. It joined the Confederate States the following month.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alabama secedes from the Union</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>'s convention voted 61 to 39 to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the fourth state to secede. Its capital Montgomery hosted the founding of the Confederate States the next month.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jefferson Davis bids farewell to the US Senate</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>'s secession, Davis and four other Southern senators gave farewell speeches and resigned. Within weeks he was chosen as President of the Confederate States.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-29">29 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Kansas enters the Union as a free state</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After the years of conflict between Free-Staters and pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> settlers known as Bleeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, Kansas entered the Union as the 34th state, a free state. The Homestead Acts of 1862 brought a further influx of settlers.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Texas votes to secede from the Union</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> convention voted 166 to 8 for secession, a decision confirmed by referendum later that month. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a> refused to swear loyalty to the Confederacy and was removed from office.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-4">4 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Seceded states meet at Montgomery to form the Confederacy</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Delegates from six seceded states opened a convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>. Within days they adopted a provisional constitution and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a> as provisional President.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-9">9 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jefferson Davis elected provisional President of the Confederacy</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Meeting in Montgomery, the Confederate provisional Congress chose Davis, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> senator and US Secretary of War. He led the Confederacy throughout the Civil War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-13">13 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Fortress of Gaeta surrenders, ending the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After a three-month siege by Piedmontese forces, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> and went into exile. Weeks later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was proclaimed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-23">23 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lincoln arrives secretly in Washington</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Warned of a plot to kill him in Baltimore, President-elect Lincoln travelled through the city overnight in secret. He was mocked for the manner of his arrival, ten days before his inauguration.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Congress creates the Nevada and Dakota territories</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Two days before leaving office, President James Buchanan signed acts organising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> and Dakota territories. Nevada's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> helped finance the Union, and it became a state in 1864.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Abraham Lincoln inaugurated as 16th President</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With seven states already seceded, Lincoln appealed to 'the better angels of our nature' in his inaugural address. War began at Fort Sumter five weeks later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-20">20 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">An earthquake destroys the city of Mendoza, Argentina</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">An earthquake with an estimated magnitude of 7.2 struck the province of Mendoza at 11:30 at night and destroyed its capital. It reached an intensity of IX–X on the Mercalli scale, from a hypocenter an estimated 30 kilometres deep.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendoza-argentina">Mendoza</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Confederate guns open fire on Fort Sumter</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Confederate batteries bombarded the federal fort in Charleston harbour, forcing its surrender the next day. The attack began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lincoln calls for militiamen to suppress the Southern rebellion</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> signed Proclamation 80, calling for 75,000 militiamen to suppress the rebellion in the states of the Confederacy. Those Southern states had seceded from the Union to preserve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; the conflict became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Virginia's secession convention votes to leave the Union</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Three days after the seizure of Fort Sumter, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Secession Convention voted to secede, on condition that a referendum approve it the next month, and to join the Confederacy. Northwestern counties loyal to the Union went on to form West Virginia.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Confederate sympathisers attack Union militia in Baltimore</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On Pratt Street, Confederate sympathisers fought Massachusetts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> militia on their way to Washington. The riot produced the first deaths of Union volunteers by hostile action in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>; attackers were killed too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-3">3 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Union troops rout Confederate forces at Philippi in Virginia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Union forces routed Confederate troops in and around Philippi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, in the clash also called the Philippi Races. It was the first organised land action of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, though it is generally viewed as a skirmish rather than a battle.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Zagonyi's Union cavalry wins the First Battle of Springfield</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Near Springfield, Missouri, Major Charles Zagonyi, commander of the bodyguard of General Frémont, won a minor Union victory over Missouri State Guard recruits through a charge widely covered by the media. Federal troops occupied the city two days later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Grant overruns a Confederate camp at Belmont, Missouri</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Union troops under Brigadier General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> overran a Confederate camp at Belmont, Missouri, but were forced to retreat when Confederate reinforcements arrived. It was Grant's first combat test of the war, against Major General Leonidas Polk.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Congress sets up the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> set up the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War to investigate the progress of the Civil War against the Confederacy. Meeting in secret but issuing reports from time to time, it was dismissed in May 1865.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">Civil War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-unification.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Italian unification, or the Risorgimento, was the political process that united the states of the Italian peninsula into a single kingdom, proclaimed in 1861.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon">Battle of Pavón</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Pavón was fought on 17 September 1861 in Santa Fe province between the forces of the State of Buenos Aires under Bartolomé Mitre and those of the Argentine Confederation under Justo José de Urquiza.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ky-hoa">Battle of Kỳ Hòa</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Kỳ Hòa was fought on 24–25 February 1861 near Saigon, when a French force under Admiral Léonard Charner stormed the Vietnamese fortified lines commanded by Nguyễn Tri Phương.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-reform-of-1861" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emancipation-reform-of-1861.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-reform-of-1861">Emancipation reform of 1861</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The emancipation reform of 1861 was the decree of Tsar Alexander II abolishing serfdom in the Russian Empire.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/canning-award">Canning Award</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Canning Award was the 1861 arbitration by Lord Canning, Governor-General of India, of the succession dispute between the sons of Sayyid Said of Oman.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fort-sumter" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fort-sumter.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fort-sumter">Battle of Fort Sumter</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Fort Sumter, on 12–13 April 1861, opened the American Civil War, when Confederate batteries bombarded the federal fort in the harbour of Charleston, South Carolina.</p></div></li></ol></section>
